    "chunk_index": 141,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "bb07a4c38cc4ebeb1dad1a79ec3945bf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1911,
    "chunk_text": "Hon. Presiding Member, I know that the Hon. Minister has been, for some time, seeking to legislate a multitude of enactments on many an aspect of the law and among such enactments, I would say, is this all-important Bill that is before us today, the Contempt of a Court, Tribunal or Institution Bill. The Bill eloquently states in its Long Title that it \"......provides for the uniform application of the law relating to the contempt of a court, tribunal or Institution...\" and also that it provides for the procedure to be followed. So, Sir, the purpose seems to be twofold: one is, to provide for a specific law on contempt and the other is, having provided for the law, to provide for the procedure to be followed in cases of contempt.\n\nWith that controversy settled, Sir, the question arises as to what this law seeks to achieve. I have looked at it very carefully and would like to say that the offence of contempt has always been in a state of uncertainty; whilst some acts amounted to contempt, the others did not. We remember the old case where the editor of a newspaper was summoned and punished for contempt for writing about judges spending their time in Nuwara Eliya. He captioned that article \u201cJustice on Holiday\u201d and pointed out, in public spirit as he claimed, that judicial time was being wasted, but the judges of yesteryear would not let him go scot-free. I remember that case in the matter of Armand de Souza, the Editor of the \"Ceylon Morning Leader\" in 1914. It was reported in Volume 18 NLR 33. But, that all-important judgment recognized one important aspect of the law of contempt of court, which states, I quote:\n\n\u201cThere is no doubt as to the right of any member of the public to criticize, and to criticize strong, judicial decisions or judicial work, and to bring to the notice of the proper authorities any charge whatsoever of the alleged misconduct on the part of a judge.\u201d"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2024-01-10-2024-01-10",
    "num": "2024-01-10",
    "date_str": "2024-01-10",
    "description": "Hansard of January 10, 2024",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/documents/hansard/1710218464098683.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2024-01-10-2024-01-10-0142",
    "chunk_index": 142,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "3dde0150cc494e2a517c6144f0691fdf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2016,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cThere is no doubt as to the right of any member of the public to criticize, and to criticize strong, judicial decisions or judicial work, and to bring to the notice of the proper authorities any charge whatsoever of the alleged misconduct on the part of a judge.\u201d\n\nSo, that being the case, this Bill now provides for some acts which would amount to contempt of court. For instance, it is set down in Clause 3(2)(a) that \"willful disobedience to any judgment, decree, direction, order, writ or other process of a court....\" will amount to contempt of court. Even a willful breach of an\n\n327 328\n\n\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dbd\u0dd2\u0dc1\u0dda\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd42\n\nundertaking given to a court will also become contemptuous under that Clause. So, what the Bill is seeking to do is to put a stututory footing to all that the judges had said in their decisions in the past. In other words, the common law principles that have been set down in judgments are being confined and codified, to some extent, in this Bill. We have no quarrel whatsoever as this legislation puts on a stututory footing the legal principles that had already been laid down by judges in this case.\n\nAnother aspect I would flag is what is meant by \"scandalizing judicial authority\", as that is sought to be made an offence. The tag \u201cscandalizing or lowering the judicial authority\u201d in Clause 3(2)(c)(i) of the Bill is, in my view, an unruly horse. Though the Supreme Court has affirmed its constitutional validity in the Bill, one cannot lose sight of the fact that the contours of \"scandalization\" are quite uncertain. The person who commits the contempt is left to the discretion and vagaries of the particular judge who hears his case. So, it is up to the adroit arguments of his counsel to take a particular act outside the ambit of scandalization. Therefore, there had been some criticisms even when this Bill was taken up on the last day. That is why the debate was adjourned for today. Therefore, we would like the Hon. Minister to have a revisit of the matter."
  },
