    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "1cb14e099f1fd6115a9d5ceb59c1f215",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1642,
    "chunk_text": "221 222\n\n\u0db4\u0dc1\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dd2\u0db1\u0dc1\u0db8\u0dca\u0dca \u0dad\u0dd4\n\nSectoral Oversight Committee. So, once again, it is very clear that the Sectoral Oversight Committee was simply used as a tool to bring this Bill forward for its Second Reading.\n\nYesterday, the Minister talked about the need to protect women and children from online abuse. Women and children are trotted out regularly by this Government as a smokescreen to hide their most devious and most dangerous measures.\n\nSo, this Bill has nothing to do with protecting women and children. Already there are laws, existing laws - even the civil law was recently amended - that anyone can take action against defamation. The necessary laws are already present in our judicial system. The problem is not the lack of laws, but the implementation of the laws. If the Minister takes the trouble to actually talk to women and children who have faced this problem in this country, who have faced not just online abuse, but even physical abuse and harassment, the treatment that is meted out by the police, the very Department that the Minister looks after, he would get an understanding that it is not the lack of laws that is preventing justice from being meted out to women and children or protection of women and children in this country, but the lack of institutional or cultural change within the police which enables them to deal with the issues of women and children in a sensitive manner. There are many stories of how women and children are\n\ntreated by the police of this country. So, this is not because of the lack of laws; this is to do with their attitude, lack of resources and lack of leadership."
  },
