    "chunk_text": "\"Every such Bill shall be referred to the relevant Sectoral Oversight Committee without a motion immediately after the First Reading. The Committee may suggest amendments to the Bill. No further proceedings to be had until the Committee has reported back to Parliament or until the expiration of period of time allowed by Parliament.\"\n\n\u201cAfter consideration, the Sectoral Oversight Committee on Media, Youth, Heritage and New Citizen, agreed to the Bill entitled \u2018Online Safety\u2019 subject to the amendments proposed by the Supreme Court Determination to the Bill and resolved that the Report of the Committee thereon be presented to Parliament by the Hon. Lalith Varna Kumara, the Chair of the Committee, on Tuesday, 23 January 2024.\u201d\n\nSo, the Report that we are supposed to consider is what I have just read. We do not know what Amendments have been made to the Bill. Officially, we have only the original Bill with 57 Clauses, of which 31 had been asked to be amended by the Supreme Court. In addition, the Sectoral Oversight Committee considered the views of an expert committee appointed by the Hon. Minister himself and in doing so, they increased the number of Amendments to 42. That means, out of the 57 Clauses, 42 are to be amended and this House is debating a Bill being in the dark with regard to those Amendments, The Sectoral Oversight Committee\u2019s Report should have given us a complete version of those Amendments, but it has not been done and instead, we are supposed to be debating this Bill, which is so controversial, without having any idea about those Amendments ."
