    "chunk_index": 111,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "5a35b9c5142037f32a548345a096112c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1841,
    "chunk_text": "Launching onto a successful economic development path demands special attention to all regions of the country, especially to the poor and vulnerable communities. Despite facing a severe economic crisis, we have consistently taken steps for the welfare of our citizens.\n\nAs of August, 2022, the President's Fund was non-functional, leaving over 9,000 applications for medical aid pending. An additional 4,000 applications were received from August to December. We addressed the backlog, disbursing Rs. 915 million to 4,917 patients throughout 2023. Now, the payment processing time has been significantly reduced, with payments made within three to five working days. Efforts are underway to increase all medical aid from 50 to 100 per cent this year. Alongside these benefits, a scholarship programme for school children has been implemented through the President's Fund.\n\nPensions have been raised while the government employees are provided with a special allowance, and steps are being taken to resolve the salary discrepancies of government retirees from 2016 to 2020 in the near future. As Government revenue increases, we will take measures to increase benefits for the people accordingly.\n\nThe tangible growth we are experiencing is evident in the current societal discussions. Not long ago, conversations centered around the challenges posed by power cuts, demanding uninterrupted electricity. Today, the discourse has shifted to electricity bills. Previously, concerns were raised about the exorbitant black market price of petrol, with talk of spending days in fuel queues. Now, the focus is on new oil companies investing in Sri Lanka. Issues related to the scarcity of vegetables have transformed into discussions about the price of carrots. The prior scarcity of paper for book printing is now replaced by discussions about VAT."
  },
