    "md5": "cb65a7b8d1b928aee8f406fc20626db0",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1218,
    "chunk_text": "\u0d9c\u0dd4\u0dab \u0da2\u0dd3.\u0da2\u0dd3. \u0db4\u0db4\u0ddc\u0db1\u0dca\u0db1\u0db8\u0dca\u0db6\u0dbd\u0db8\u0dca \u0db8\u0da2\u0dad\u0dcf (\u0bae\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 \u0b9c\u0bc0.\u0b9c\u0bc0. \u0ba4\u0baa\u0bbe\u0ba9\u0bcd\u0ba9\u0bae\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bb2\u0bae\u0bcd) (The Hon. G.G. Ponnambalam) I thank you, Hon. Presiding Member, for giving me some time to participate in this Debate, considering the present situation facing the country.\n\nSir, if you take the economy of the North and the East, to a great extent, it depends on fisheries. Also, if you take Sri Lanka\u2019s coastline, well over 60 per cent of it is found in the North and the East because of its geographical nature or the way in which the North and the East is formed. Therefore, naturally, fisheries is a major sector in the economy of the area. Whilst this is the case, Sir, during the 30 years of war, fishing was banned in the North and the East. It was only during the peace process that there was some relaxation of the ban for the fishermen to go a couple of kilometres out into the sea, but more or less, it was banned even then. Under those circumstances, at the end of the war, those fisher folk were trying to stand up on their own feet; finally, they felt that they could get back on their feet.\n\n691 692\n\n\u0db4\u0daf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dbd\u0dd2\u0dc1\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dca \u0db8\u0dd2\u0dc0"
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2024-02-21-2024-02-21",
