    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/documents/hansard/1711444651040085.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2024-02-21-2024-02-21-0142",
    "chunk_index": 142,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "6d899bee6cd3aab155003523d5fce0e3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1995,
    "chunk_text": "Sir, if you take the economy of the North and the East, to a great extent, it depends on fisheries. Also, if you take Sri Lanka\u2019s coastline, well over 60 per cent of it is found in the North and the East because of its geographical nature or the way in which the North and the East is formed. Therefore, naturally, fisheries is a major sector in the economy of the area. Whilst this is the case, Sir, during the 30 years of war, fishing was banned in the North and the East. It was only during the peace process that there was some relaxation of the ban for the fishermen to go a couple of kilometres out into the sea, but more or less, it was banned even then. Under those circumstances, at the end of the war, those fisher folk were trying to stand up on their own feet; finally, they felt that they could get back on their feet.\n\n691 692\n\n\u0db4\u0daf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dbd\u0dd2\u0dc1\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dca \u0db8\u0dd2\u0dc0\n\nSurely, there is something wrong here! This is a Navy which, at every opportunity, boasts that they managed to destroy the LTTE and the LTTE\u2019s naval capacity. Well, here, you have a situation where you have strengthened the Navy\u2019s presence in the North and the East, When the LTTE was there, you did not have this presence; today, you have strengthened their presence in the North and the East. You are getting so many donations from various other navies, various other countries to strengthen your naval capacity and despite that, these foreign fishermen are allowed to come. It is because they are allowed to come, not because of anything else. So, that is the reality, Sir. During the war, there was a ban on fishing. Then, after the war, you are deliberately allowing foreign fishermen to come in and destroy the assets of the local fishermen in the North and the East. It cannot be any other reason than to deliberately destroy their existence, their livelihood so that they would leave that land and then, you could grab that land, which you already illegally occupy. That has to be the only reason."
  },
