    "chunk_id": "2024-02-22-2024-02-22-0085",
    "chunk_index": 85,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "08275b6d60bfee88e1f89274ac0c20f5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2060,
    "chunk_text": "It saddened me at the way Sri Lanka behaved as a society upon his death and that, we must address as community leaders. Regardless of whatever political ideology one shares, whoever he supports in the realm of politics and regardless of who he likes or do not like, every human being deserves compassion. According to our Constitution, we give the first and foremost place to Buddhism. So, I do not think what happened upon his death is what Buddhism teaches us, to show insensitivity to fellow human beings, let alone after they die. Therefore, it is something that must be addressed.\n\nThough the late Hon. Sanath Nishantha and myself come from different political backgrounds, hold different political ideologies and different political dynamics, one thing I must recognize. That is, in his tenure as the State Minister of Water Supply, he was completely dedicated to providing water to the rural areas. He had worked closely with 5,000 community-based organizations, which I was unware of before his death. Had I known that, then he would have been celebrated even more. But, the reality of the matter is, today, we have lost an efficient administrator.\n\nToday, one thing that Sri Lanka, especially the youth of this country, must reflect on is not to be instigated by certain political entities into becoming something that is not humane. We must understand the fact that the late\n\n819 820\n\n[\u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0dd3\u0dbb\u0dc3\u0dd4\u0db8\u0db1 \u0dd3\u0dbb\u0dc3\u0dd2\u0dcf\u0d82\u0dc4 \u0db8\u0dc4\u0dad\u0dcf]\n\n2024 \u0dc1\u0db4\u0db3\u0dbb\u0dc0\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dd2 22\n\nHon. Sanath Nishantha being a Parliamentarian, a State Minister, a loyalist of the SLPP, all that is secondary. What is primary is the fact that he was a husband and a father and that needs to be looked at.\n\nSir, today, I stand before all of you, on behalf of his family, to thank all the Hon. Members of Parliament who have spoken so far and who will speak after me. One thing we, as humans, must realize is that we really do not value someone when he is living, but we understand his loss only after he is dead. And, that is something that must change within us."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2024-02-22-2024-02-22",
