    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/documents/hansard/1711536075022920.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2024-02-22-2024-02-22-0086",
    "chunk_index": 86,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "25a5300621edf5bbee39787540167f3f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1942,
    "chunk_text": "Sir, today, I stand before all of you, on behalf of his family, to thank all the Hon. Members of Parliament who have spoken so far and who will speak after me. One thing we, as humans, must realize is that we really do not value someone when he is living, but we understand his loss only after he is dead. And, that is something that must change within us.\n\nApart from that, I would also like to take this opportunity to explain the aftermath of the accident that took his life. I was shocked to find out how certain people had spoken ill of him. I was even more shocked to find out how many people had shared the visuals of his death. What kind of animal do you have to be to look at another human being in that manner? This is not the society we want our children to grow up in; this is not the society we want to carve out as community leaders.\n\nSir, today, I am sitting in this Parliament as the youngest Cabinet Minister in the history of Sri Lanka; today, I am sitting here looking at the very dark future because if it keeps going on in this manner, I can assure you, not only will we lose our children, but will also lose Sri Lanka eventually, which is why I would like to urge all the Hon. Members of this House not to politicize his death, but to recognize the efforts made by a man who was committed to his ideologies and to his family. If this were to happen to any one of those Hon. Members, I do not think they would want their families to feel the way the late Hon. Sanath Nishantha\u2019s family feels even now.\n\nI would like to tell his children that, at the end of the day, their father was somebody who was committed to his task and his family, regardless of what anybody says, and not only was he committed, but was also a genuine human being who stuck to his beliefs. As a politician, he did not say what everyone wanted to hear; he said what everyone needed to hear. With those comments, Sir, I would like to end my speech."
  },
